# SFDMU v5 export.json

Full detail: `CLAUDE.md` and `docs/references/sfdmu-composite-key-optimizations.md`.

## externalId

Use `;` delimiters (`Field1;Field2`). The v4 `$$Field1$Field2` syntax is invalid here.
`$$` composite **column names inside CSVs** remain correct for Upsert target matching.

## Operation choice

| Situation | Pattern |
|---|---|
| Direct unique field | `Upsert`, `externalId: Name` |
| Composite of direct fields | `Upsert`, `externalId: Field1;Field2`, `$$Field1$Field2` CSV column |
| externalId uses any relationship traversal | `Insert` + `deleteOldData: true` |
| Auto-number Name, all-relationship externalId | `Insert` + `deleteOldData: true` |
| Empty CSV (no records yet) | `excluded: true` |

## Never silently switch Upsert to Insert + deleteOldData

`deleteOldData: true` is destructive — it deletes all existing records before inserting.
Before proposing the change you must explain which specific v5 bug applies, confirm no
direct-field externalId exists, and get **explicit user approval**. When in doubt keep
`Upsert` and explain the trade-off.

Customer demo plans deliberately avoid global `deleteOldData` on `RateCardEntry`,
`RateAdjustmentByTier`, `ProductUsageResourcePolicy`, and `ProductUsageGrant` so non-demo
org data survives. Scoped Apex deletes handle the demo slice instead.

## Ordering

SFDMU deletes `deleteOldData: true` objects in **reverse array order**. Order objects parent
to child so deletions run child to parent and satisfy FK constraints.

## SELECT clauses

Include every `ORDER BY` field in the SELECT. Relationship traversal columns must match what
the CSV headers expect. For `Insert` operations on pricing objects, use direct ID fields —
traversal fields get stripped ("Referenced field removed") and break FK resolution.

